;####################
;# MISC INFORMATION #
;####################
@NODE MiscInfo "Phoenix documentation - Misc Information"
@{b}The size of project@{ub}
Per 18 October 1995 the Phoenix project consist of 400kb C source code
and 20kb Arexx script.

@{b}Version numbers@{ub}
The whole project has a version and revision number. The version number
tells the full release number. The revision number tells the beta-
release or sub-release number.

Each module also has its own version and revision number. The version
number should be the same has the project version number, while the
revision number is the "version" number of the module.

Beware! E.g. 1.5 and 1.50 isn't the same thing. 1.50 is much higher than
1.5.
@ENDNODE

;######################
;# THE EXPORT MODULES #
;######################
@NODE Export "Phoenix documentation - Export modules"
Phoenix currently supports three offline formats:

   @{" QWK spesific issues          " Link ExportQwk}
   @{" Bluewave spesific issues     " Link ExportBW}
   @{" MBBS spesific issues         " Link ExportMBBS}

General export information:

   @{" CLI arguments/usage          " Link ExportArguments}
   @{" Twit/kill filter             " Link ExportTwit}
   @{" Limit the packet size        " Link ExportLimitSize}
   @{" Background packet generating " Link ExportAsync}

@ENDNODE

;###########################
;# EXPORTERS CLI ARGUMENTS #
;###########################
@NODE ExportArguments "Phoenix documentation - Export CLI arguments/usage"
The modules uses dos.library's ReadArgs() to parse the CLI arguments.
I.e. modules should not be launched from Workbench, unless you use
a script.

The ReadArgs() template is:
   USER/A,CHARSET=CS/K,NOOUTPUT=NO/S,ERRORQUIET=EQ/S,SETRC=RC/S

For more information about ReadArgs() templates see the ReadArgs
section in this document.

USER
   This argument must be supplied.

   Name of the user which the export module should generate a mail
   packet for.

CHARSET or CS
   This argument is optional.

   Name of the charset to translate messages and bulletins with.
   The charset file must be in Zeus:Config/Translation/ for Zeus
   to load it. Export modules uses the OUTPUT table.

NOOUTPUT or NO
   This argument is optional.

   If this argument is supplied, The export module will surpress all
   standard output (Not errors).

ERRORQUIET or EQ
   This argument is optional.

   If this argument is supplied, The export module will surpress all
   error messages (Not standard output).

SETRC or RC
   This argument is optional.

   Please do not use this argument since it's obsolete, and probably
   will be removed soon.

@ENDNODE

;###############
;# TWIT FILTER #
;###############
@NODE ExportTwit "Phoenix documentation - Twit/kill filter"
The export modules supports twit filter/kill filter. It's an AmigaDOS
wildcard pattern set by the user. If an author of a message match this
pattern the export module will not export the message.

@ENDNODE

;#####################
;# LIMIT PACKET SIZE #
;#####################
@NODE ExportLimitSize "Phoenix documentation - Limit the packet size"
Mail packets can be limited by max number of messages per conference
and packet. This can both users and the SysOp set (A user can't set
a higher figure than the SysOp).

The SysOp can also limit the actual size of a packet (in bytes).
This can be usefull if you have the work directory in RAM.

Beware that the size limit only applies to the message file, not other
files like NEWFILES.DAT etc.

Also note that 0 DOES NOT mean unlimited!

@ENDNODE

;#########################
;# ASYNCHRONOUS GRABBING #
;#########################
@NODE ExportAsync "Phoenix documentation - Asynchronous grabbing"
The user can select if they want to watch the packing of mail packets
or it should be done in the background. They can even abort or end a
background grab.

When the packet is finished, or the packet failed, the user is
notified via a node message (No matter which node the user is on).
If the user isn't online the message will be written to his/her
log.

Background packing can be turned on/off with the "C" option in
Phoenix's menu. The packet can be aborted, or ended, with the "E"
option.

@ENDNODE


;#####################
;# QWK EXPORT MODULE #
;#####################
@NODE ExportQwk "Phoenix documentation - Qwk export module"
QwkExport is an export module under the Phoenix offline mail system,
which generates Qwk compatible mail packets.

Despite Qwk's many limitation it's the most popular offline mail
format today. The Qwk format was invented by Mark Herring in 1987.
For more information read the documents refered to below.

   @{" The files in a QWK packet, and what they are.               " Link ExportQwkFiles}
   @{" Limitations in the QWK format, and how Phoenix avoids them. " Link QwkLimits}
   @{" QwkExport errors and return codes.                          " Link ExportQwkErrors}
   @{" References to QWK documentation.                            " Link QwkReference}

General export information:

   @{" CLI arguments/usage                                         " Link ExportArguments}
   @{" Twit/kill filter                                            " Link ExportTwit}
   @{" Limit the packet size                                       " Link ExportLimitSize}
   @{" Background packet generating                                " Link ExportAsync}

@ENDNODE

;####################
;# QWK EXPORT FILES #
;####################
@NODE ExportQwkFiles "Phoenix documentation - Files in a qwk packet"
QwkExport allways generates the following files:

@{b}MESSAGES.DAT@{ub}
   Contains all the message text and headers.

@{b}CONTROL.DAT@{ub}
   Contains information about your BBS, the user, and all your
   conferences.

   QwkExport get the BBS name, location and SysOp's name from Zeus'
   configuration.

   Beware: According to the QWK spesifications conference names
   shouldn't be longer than 13 chars. QwkExport doesn't care about
   this since most offliners can handle longer names. If this is a
   problem for you, please contact us, and we will compile a special
   version.

@{b}DOOR.ID@{ub}
   Contains door name and version, BBS version, and information about
   which control types your BBS supports.

   Control types lets your user change setings on the BBS and in
   Phoenix by sending a message to the control name you specified
   in PhoenixCfg.

   See the PhoenixCfg documentation for more information about control
   types.

QwkExport may generate/include the following files:

@{b}NEWFILES.DAT@{ub}
   A list of new files on your BBS. This may either be the file you
   specified in PhoenixCfg->Paths->file list or generated by
   QwkExport's internal new files generator.

   To make QwkExport generate such a list you must set an env variable
   called "PNF_<user name>" (Note: Not with under score). The variable
   must contain when the user user last scanned new files. The date
   format is seconds since 1 Januar 1978 in ASCII.

@{b}ddd.NDX@{ub}
   This is only generated if the user's wants index files. ddd is the
   number of a conference and contains pointers to the messages in
   MESSAGES.DAT. Amiga offliners usally ignores these files, however
   many PC offliners needs them.

@{b}PERSONAL.NDX@{ub}
   This file is in the same format as ddd.NDX, however it contains
   pointers to messages addressed to user.

@{b}HELLO.TXT@{ub}
   The file you specified in PhoenixCfg->Paths->Hello. It's only
   included if the user wants it.

@{b}GOODBYE.TXT@{ub}
   The file you specified in PhoenixCfg->Paths->Goodbye. It's only
   included if the user wants it.

@{b}NEWS.TXT@{ub}
   The file you specified in PhoenixCfg->Paths->News. It's only
   included if the user wants it.

@{b}BLT-0.ddd@{ub}
   These files are new bulletins since the user last logged on. ddd is
   the number of the bulletin. They're only included if the user wants
   them.

@ENDNODE

;################
;# QWK's LIMITS #
;################
@NODE QwkLimits "Phoenix documentation - Qwk's limitations"
As mentioned before Qwk isn't a perfect format. Some of it's limits
are:

Names and subject
   ...can be max 25 chars long. We will probably try to solve this
   problem later on. Note that QwkImport allready have partly solved
   it.

   Names should be in upper case, according to the spesifications, so
   don't blame us. :-)

Conference names
   ...can max be 13 chars long, however QwkExport doesn't care. :-)

Conference numbers
   ...must not be above 9999. I'm serious, this will probably mess up
   messages.

Netmail
   ...isn't supported by Qwk. However QwkImport can handle replies.

@ENDNODE

;#####################
;# QWK EXPORT ERRORS #
;#####################
@NODE ExportQwkErrors "Phoenix documentation - Errors and return codes"
QwkExport returns with the error code. I.e. if everything went ok it
returns 0. In an Arexx script the return code is stored in the RC
variable after "Address Command" or "CLIDoor".

Note that QwkExport sets an exclusive (write) lock on the
work sub-directory, so if it crash while generating mail you can't
delete it unless you reboot.

Currently QwkExport has these error codes/messages:

Code.......: 1
Message....: Wrong arguments
Description: You supplied wrong arguments, either keywords that
             QwkExport doesn't understand or too few.

Code.......: 2
Message....: Can't open zeus.library
Description: Check that zeus.library is in libs: or zeus:libs/.

Code.......: 3
Message....: Can't open zmf.library
Description: Check that zmf.library is in libs: or zeus:libs/.

Code.......: 4
Message....: Can't get shared data
Description: Zeus' main server is probably not running.

Code.......: 5
Message....: Message server isn't running
Description: Zeus' message server is not running.

Code.......: 6
Message....: Can't get message shared data
Description: Zeus' message server is probably not running. This error
             should not happend, since QwkExport check if it's running.

Code.......: 7
Message....: Can't load the user
Description: The user you specified doesn't exists on the BBS, or
             QwkExport was unable to load him/her.

Code.......: 8
Message....: Can't load user's XData
Description: The user's account is probably corrupt.

Code.......: 9
Message....: Can't load user's Phoenix configuration
Description: Phoenix stores a user's configuration in XData (Tag: PNXM).
             QwkExport can't create this by it self. See the Phoenix
             Arexx interface documentation for more information.

Code.......: 10
Message....: Can't open zabbs.library
Description: zabbs.library is a library that is supplied with Phoenix.
             Please check that it is in libs: or Zeus:libs/.

             zabbs.library is shared by both Phoenix and one of our
             other projects, ZABBS.

Code.......: 11
Message....: Can't get shared file data
Description: Zeus' file server was probably not running.

Code.......: 13
Message....: Can't load Phoenix's configuration
Description: Check that Zeus:Config/Phoenix/Phoenix.Cfg exists. If not
             run PhoenixCfg and save.

Code.......: 14
Message....: There exists unarchived mail
Description: QwkExport doesn't archive the mail packet it generates.
             Delete the old mail packet (<work directory>/<user_name>).

             This error occures when QwkExport can't create the
             <user_name> sub directory, so the reason could be that the
             work directory doesn't exists.

Code.......: 15
Message....: Mail packet aborted
Description: The user hitted CTRL-C or lost carrier. QwkExport will then
             stop packing and delete what it have created so far.

Code.......: 16
Message....: Error writing MESSAGES.DAT
Description: This error only happends if QwkExport couldn't create the
             MESSAGES.DAT file.

Code.......: 17
Message....: Error writing CONTROL.DAT
Description: This error only happends if QwkExport couldn't create the
             CONTROL.DAT file.

@ENDNODE

